1
00:00:00,561 --> 00:00:02,977
Yesterday's scan found delivery_summary.

2
00:00:02,977 --> 00:00:04,862
Today's scan does not mention it.

3
00:00:04,862 --> 00:00:06,355
Has the table been deleted?

4
00:00:06,355 --> 00:00:09,028
Pause and name another explanation before deciding.

5
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,314
The collector might have lost permission,

6
00:00:16,314 --> 00:00:19,001
scanned the wrong database, or stopped early.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,847
Keep the last successful observation visible, with current status unverified.

8
00:00:23,847 --> 00:00:28,774
Confirm deletion using supporting evidence, such as a recorded drop operation or a

9
00:00:28,774 --> 00:00:31,672
complete scan that was able and expected to see the table.

10
00:00:32,545 --> 00:00:35,638
Now use the page's separate example from twenty first October.

11
00:00:35,638 --> 00:00:41,072
Scheduler event E20 reports that run R1000 published summary-R1000 at ten.

12
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:45,765
The catalogue receives it at one minute past ten, then again at four minutes past ten.

13
00:00:45,765 --> 00:00:48,204
The source and stable event identifier identify

14
00:00:48,204 --> 00:00:50,859
one reported publication received twice.

15
00:00:50,859 --> 00:00:53,589
Matching message text alone would not establish that.

16
00:00:54,463 --> 00:00:57,537
At six minutes past ten, event E19 arrives.

17
00:00:57,537 --> 00:01:02,753
It reports that a different run, R0950, failed at nine fifty.

18
00:01:02,753 --> 00:01:04,855
Move it to the earlier run's history.

19
00:01:04,855 --> 00:01:08,757
Receiving that message last cannot make R1000 a failure.

20
00:01:08,757 --> 00:01:11,585
Keep event time and receipt time separate; clocks

21
00:01:11,585 --> 00:01:13,808
alone need not establish order across systems.

22
00:01:14,812 --> 00:01:18,727
The scheduler reports summary-R1000 published at ten.

23
00:01:18,727 --> 00:01:22,200
But database scan S7 observes the table labelled

24
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:26,367
summary-R0940 at three minutes past ten.

25
00:01:26,367 --> 00:01:29,147
Which output does the table actually contain?

26
00:01:29,147 --> 00:01:32,634
Explain what is known, and what still needs investigation.

27
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:34,487
Pause for more time if you need it.

28
00:01:39,292 --> 00:01:41,818
These records do not settle which claim is correct.

29
00:01:41,818 --> 00:01:46,523
Show the current published version as disputed, keeping both sources and their times.

30
00:01:46,523 --> 00:01:49,188
The version label could be wrong, or the scheduler

31
00:01:49,188 --> 00:01:51,528
could have reported publication incorrectly.

32
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:54,057
Recognising a duplicate and placing the delayed

33
00:01:54,057 --> 00:01:56,317
failure correctly do not resolve this disagreement.

34
00:01:57,375 --> 00:02:00,012
Check write logs and saved output versions.

35
00:02:00,012 --> 00:02:02,460
If that investigation resolves the disagreement,

36
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:05,124
record the evidence alongside the conclusion.

37
00:02:05,124 --> 00:02:07,860
One reported publication, earlier run history,

38
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:10,533
and disputed current state can all coexist.

39
00:02:10,533 --> 00:02:14,155
Choosing whichever message arrived last would hide the reason to investigate.

40
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:16,940
Try the page's map exercise.

41
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:21,229
Open Source observation's Example record, then Collection run.

42
00:02:21,229 --> 00:02:26,565
These map records are from twentieth October, not the event example we just used.

43
00:02:26,565 --> 00:02:29,108
Compare observation and collection times.

44
00:02:29,108 --> 00:02:32,413
If tomorrow's scan omits the dataset, explain

45
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:34,698
which checks you need before calling it deleted.

46
00:02:34,698 --> 00:02:36,575
Then use Check your answer.
